To achieve smooth, distortion-free video warping, the vertex shader itself needs to be incredibly lightweight. The trick to projection mapping is not doing the heavy math in the shader, but rather feeding the shader a heavily subdivided grid.If you only use four vertices (a single quad) and try to warp the corners, WebGL will use linear interpolation for the texture. This creates "affine distortion," where the video looks pinched or folded along the diagonal seam. To fix this, you must generate a dense mesh (e.g., a 20x20 grid of triangles).Here is the WebGL architecture for a distortion-free mesh warping pipeline.1. The Vertex ShaderBecause your React and SVG dashboard handles the complex bezier curves and node math, the shader only needs to receive the final warped coordinates and match them to the original video dimensions.OpenGL Shading Languageattribute vec2 a_warped_position; // The dragged X/Y coordinates in clip space (-1.0 to 1.0)
attribute vec2 a_uv_coord;        // Original 0.0 to 1.0 video texture coordinates

varying vec2 v_uv_coord;

void main() {
    // WebGL requires coordinates in clip space. 
    // We pass the pre-calculated warped positions directly.
    gl_Position = vec4(a_warped_position, 0.0, 1.0);
    
    // Pass the original UV coordinates to the fragment shader for texture mapping
    v_uv_coord = a_uv_coord;
}
2. The Fragment ShaderThe fragment shader takes the interpolated UV coordinates from the dense grid and samples the current video frame. Because the mesh is highly subdivided, the texture maps smoothly without pinching.OpenGL Shading Languageprecision mediump float;

// Received from the vertex shader
varying vec2 v_uv_coord;

// The current video frame uploaded to the GPU
uniform sampler2D u_video_texture;

void main() {
    // Look up the color of the video pixel at the specified coordinate
    gl_FragColor = texture2D(u_video_texture, v_uv_coord);
}
3. The JavaScript Data FlowTo make this performant on a smartphone or a dedicated render node, you decouple the UI interaction from the GPU pipeline:Static UV Buffer: When the app loads, you generate a 20x20 grid. You upload the a_uv_coord array to the GPU once. These never change, representing the flat, perfect video file.Dynamic Vertex Buffer: You create a second array for a_warped_position. Initially, these match the flat grid.The Bridge: When you drag an SVG node in your interface, JavaScript calculates how that movement pulls the surrounding grid points (using an interpolation algorithm like Bilinear Interpolation or Inverse Distance Weighting).The Update Loop: Instead of destroying and recreating the WebGL geometry, you use gl.bufferSubData() to overwrite the existing a_warped_position array in the GPU's memory. This is blisteringly fast and allows for real-time 60fps manipulation, even with complex bezier masks.